\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 Empirical landscapes \endash  species distribution models\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 To obtain the maps where climate is suitable for each of the species we performed species distribution models. We used presence data on 50 km x 50 km UTM grid cells ({Mitchell-Jones {\i et\~al.}}, 1999) within Europe from -10\u194?\u186? 9\u226?\u128?\u153? 23\u226?\u128?\u153?\u226?\u128?\u153? \u226?\u128?\u147? 30\u194?\u186? 43\u226?\u128?\u153? 10\u226?\u128?\u153?\u226?\u128?\u153? E and from 34\u194?\u186? 59\u226?\u128?\u153? 30\u226?\u128?\u153?\u226?\u128?\u153? \u226?\u128?\u147? 70\u194?\u186? 58\u226?\u128?\u153? 33\u226?\u128?\u153?\u226?\u128?\u153? N. We modelled the distribution of each species with four climatic variables of the period 1961-1990: mean temperature of the coldest month, mean annual summed precipitation, mean annual growing degree days ({>} 5\u194?\u186?C), and a moisture summer index calculated as potential evapotranspiration divided by precipitation (mean values of the 6 months between April and September). Future climatic variables were obtained considering the global climate model CNRM-CM5 and the representative concentration pathway (RCPs) 4.5 for the period 2071-2100, i.e., projections that are recorded in the Fifth Assessment IPCC report (http://www.worldclim.org/). The resolution for future projections was \~14 km x 14 km. An ensemble of models was generated for each species. The ensemble included projections with seven methods: generalized linear models (GLM), generalized additive models (GAM), generalized boosting models (GBM), classification tree analysis (CTA), artificial neural networks (ANN), flexible discriminant analysis (FDA), and surface range envelope (SRE) ({Ara{\'fa}jo {\i et\~al.}}, 2011). Models were calibrated for the baseline (1961\u226?\u128?\u147?1990) using 80% random sample of the initial data and evaluated against the remaining 20{% data, using the area under the curve (AUC) of the receiver operation characteristic (ROC) and the true skill statistic (TSS) ({Liu {\i et\~al.}}, 2011). Projections were performed 10 times, each time selecting a different 80% random sample while verifying model accuracy against the remaining 20%. The evaluation statistics were used to consider the possibility of exclusion of models in the ensemble approach on the basis of poor matching between predictions and observations. Here, models with AUC {<} 0.8 or TSS values {<} 0.6 would be removed from the analysis. For the final assessment, models were calibrated using 100% of the species distribution data as it has been shown that random removal of presence records adds a non-trivial amount of uncertainty in future projections ({Araujo {\i et\~al.}}, 2009). Given the projections obtained with the seven models, we calculated an ensemble model for each species. The ensemble was based on a weighted mean probability of occurrence per species and per grid cell with a proportional decay, where weights are obtained from the AUC and TSS obtained on the evaluation data ({Marmion {\i et\~al.}}, 2009). Ensemble projections were transformed into suitable/unsuitable using the TSS as the binary method ({Thuiller {\i et\~al.}}, 2013). All models were run in {\f2 R} (R Core Team 2014) using default options of the {\i biomod2} package ({Thuiller {\i et\~al.}}, 2013).\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi360 We use the data from three European mammal species: European Hare ({\i Lepus europaeus}), Flying Squirrel ({\i Pteromys volans}) and Chamois ({\i Rupicapra rupicapra})). These three species were chosen as they, based on the calculations described above, gain potential climate space outside their range, meaning they have future landscapes that where habitat and climate are suitable and as such could be colonized. Notable is that this is not the case for all species as they can undergo total range contractions instead.\par
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 Comparision simulated and empirical landscapes\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 In order to evaluate the ecological relevance of the generated landscapes are we compare them to the empirical data. We do this comparison by calculating the aggregation of suitable habitat in the generated and empirical landscapes respectively using the Clark and Evans aggregation index ({Clark & Evans}, 1954).\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi360 The empirical data used here describe the distribution of suitable habitats after climate change for three European mammal species used to justify the dispersal kernels (see section \ldblquote Empirical landscapes \endash  species distribution models\rdblquote  above). The empirical data is raster format and the generated landscapes are point pattern data. In order to do a comparison we therefore do some modifications to the two data sets. There are in total 410 50 km grid-cells (summed over all three species) with projected suitable land. In the empirical data set all habitat patches (1km cells), within each 50 km grid-cell, that are both climatically and land-use suitable where set to 1, all other grid-cells set to 0. However, some of these grid-cells contain a very low number of habitat patches, e.g., if the grid-cell is in the edge of the distribution. All grid-cells with less then 20 habitat patches were removed (as it is irrelevant to compare the simulated landscapes with empirical with such a low coverage) leaving us with 297 empirical landscapes to analyze. The number of habitat patches in those landscape range from 100 to 594. We then use the R-package {\i spatstat} ({Turner {\i et\~al.}}, 2015) and transform these landscapes from raster to point pattern. For illustration of one example landscape see Fig. S1.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi360 The generated landscapes are in point-pattern format. Each habitat patch (point) is represented by x- and y-coordinates in a squared coordinate system where each side is described by the range 0 to 1. Some points can be located very close to each other and will thereby act as a single patch, even for species with the most restricted dispersal. Therefore we merge those points that have coordinates with three or more decimals equal for both the x- and y-coordinate. The number of habitat patches in those modified landscapes then range from 113 to 420.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi360 These slightly modified empirical and generated landscapes are then compared. The Clark and Evans aggregation index ({Clark & Evans}, 1954) is calculated for both sets. This comparison gives a rough estimate of the spread of aggregation in the simulated and empirical landscapes respectively. We find that the measurements coincide and that the distribution of habitats in our generated landscapes are well represented in the empirical data (Fig. S2).\par
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 Calculating dispersal kernels for empirical data\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 A species dispersal kernel shows the probability distribution of the distance traveled by any individual. As a basis for the model we use dispersal data for eight European mammals ({Whitmee & Orme}, 2013). The species\u226?\u128?\u153? natal dispersal distance was used to calculate the dispersal kernels for a landscape of size 50x50 km. The dispersal data used is the distance where the 99% percentile of dispersed individuals is found. We start the calculations by normalizing the data, e.g., if 99% dispersal for a species is 17.3 km and the landscape size 50x50 km then the normalized values for dispersal is 17.3/50 = 0.346 in a 1x1 landscape. Equation for the dispersal kernel is \par
{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx4552\tqr\tx9104\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i p}({\i d})={\i e}\\s\\up6({\fs20 \u8722?({\i d}/{\i a})})}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 (1)}\par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 assuming the shape parameter {{\i b}} is 1 (see Eq.2 in main text). Since the dispersal distance {{\i d}} here is the distance where 99% of dispersers are found within, the integral up until this distance will be equal to the infinite integral multiplied by 0.99. This expression gives the following equation:\par
{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx4552\tqr\tx9104\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i F}\\s\\do6({\fs20 {\i d}})\u8722?{\i F}\\s\\do6({\fs20 0})=({\i F}\\s\\do6({\fs20 {\i x}})\u8722?{\i F}\\s\\do6({\fs20 0})){\u215*}0.99}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 (2)}\par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 where {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i F}\\s\\do6({\fs20 {\i d}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} is is dispersal for a certain distance {{\i d}} , {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i F}\\s\\do6({\fs20 0})}}{\fldrslt }}
} is equal to {{\i a}}, and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i F}\\s\\do6({\fs20 {\i x}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} is when distance goes towards infinity. This gives the following equation to solve:\par
{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx4552\tqr\tx9104\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \u8722?{\i a}*{\i e}\\s\\up6({\fs20 \u8722?({\i d}/{\i a})})+{\i a}=(\u8722?{\i a}*{\i e}\\s\\up6({\fs20 \u8722?({\i x}/{\i a})})+{\i a})*0.99}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 (3)}\par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 when x is large. Solving this expression numerically yields an {{\i a}}-value for the species and from that the dispersal kernel can be derived using Eq. S1.\par
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 Classes of heterogeneous landscapes\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 The high variation in population densities between the replicates in heterogeneous landscapes (Fig. 5 in main text) urged a closer examination of the actual distribution of habitat patches in those landscapes. When the habitat patches are distributed in a more aggregated manner this means that there are large areas where no (or very few) habitats are placed and reaching new habitats thereby demand long dispersal. To see the effects of these differences in species survival we divided the heterogeneous landscapes in three classes based on the fraction of habitat positioned in middle third of the landscape: A - 0\endash 23%, B) 23\endash 43% and C) 43\endash 100%(for visualization see Fig. S3). The number of replicates belonging to each class was 129, 137 and 94 respectively. These classes were then used in the regression tree analyses to further clarify the importance of the actual distribution of habitat.\par
